# Markdown Documentation Gates

This rule owns mandatory Markdown policy only. Use the [documentation standards skill](file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/skills/documentation-standards/SKILL.md) for document design, Mermaid syntax, templates, ADRs, and writing workflows.

## Structure and Proportionality

- Start standalone documents with one clear H1 and use a logical heading hierarchy without skipped levels.
- Add a table of contents, conclusion, alert, or diagram only when the document's complexity benefits from it. Do not force ceremony onto a short note or changelog entry.
- Use lists for sequences or sets and tables only when comparison across aligned columns is materially clearer.
- Avoid decorative emojis in professional technical documentation.

## Prose and Code

- Keep each prose paragraph on one source line and let the renderer soft-wrap it. Do not hard-wrap prose at a fixed column.
- Break lists, tables, front matter, code, and diagrams only as their syntax requires.
- Use fenced code blocks with the correct language identifier and complete, safe examples.
- Do not use comments, headings, or callouts as decoration or to repeat nearby prose.

## Links and Navigation

- Navigational URLs and repository paths must be clickable Markdown links with descriptive text; do not wrap links intended for navigation in backticks.
- Ordinary project documentation uses portable repository-relative links for same-repository navigation.
- Handbook-internal cross-file links use `file:///Users/Devesh_Padmanabhan/.cursor/agent-engineering-handbook/...` so agents working in another repository resolve the correct source.
- Literal URL or path values shown as configuration, syntax, logs, or examples may use inline code.
- Verify changed links and anchors.

## Alerts and Diagrams

- Use only supported GitHub alerts: `NOTE`, `TIP`, `IMPORTANT`, `WARNING`, and `CAUTION`.
- Keep alerts concise and place them next to the relevant instruction.
- Use Mermaid only when a relationship, sequence, state, hierarchy, or timeline is clearer visually than in short prose.
- Keep diagrams focused, label nodes clearly, quote labels containing punctuation, and avoid explicit theme colors.
- Verify Mermaid syntax in a compatible renderer before completion.

## Accessibility and Verification

- Use descriptive link text and image alt text; do not encode meaning through color alone.
- Keep tables readable on narrow screens and avoid unnecessarily wide columns.
- Run the repository's Markdown lint and link checks for changed documentation.
